{
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The reroute was traffic-justified, so the driver's turn onto Upper Serangoon Road was a reaction to an incident on his own route and not a detour of his own choosing. EV-ADVIS-05005-01 is an LTA traffic-incident record for a VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN on the Kallang-Paya Lebar Expressway, the expressway the recommended route runs on (optimal_route_via: Punggol Central, TPE, KPE, Paya Lebar Road), with a validity window of 09:59:38 to 10:54:38 that covers the trip (10:14:38 to 10:38:08). POL-RD-23 requires only a traffic advisory that intersects the recommended route and overlaps the trip window, and its admissible 'incident' vocabulary expressly includes BREAKDOWN and VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN. The rider's framing of the turn as an unexplained deviation is therefore wrong on the face of the record.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-ADVIS-05005-01", "EV-TRIP-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-23"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-ADVIS-05005-01].payload.derived.v7.justification_kind",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The driver told the rider, in the app, what he was doing and why, and the rider accepted it. MSG-05005-003 - 'Sorry ah, KPE got breakdown inside the tunnel so I go Upper Serangoon Road instead' - was sent at 10:24:30, delivered, and read by the rider, who answered 'ok noted' at 10:25:00 (MSG-05005-004). POL-RD-21 accepts in-app chat as the channel for this notice, requires only that the driver give 'a brief reason', and expressly provides that where the chat log confirms the rider's acknowledgment 'the deviation is agreed'. This log confirms it. The rider raised nothing at the time and nothing during the thirteen minutes of trip that remained after his reply; his assertion that he 'had no say' is contradicted by his own reply in the same log.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-05005", "EV-TRIP-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-21"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-CHAT-05005].payload.messages",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "With the reroute traffic-justified under POL-RD-23 and the rider's acknowledgment confirmed in the chat log under POL-RD-21 - which makes the deviation an agreed one - the row that fits this case is POL-RD-24(a), and no refund is due. The deviation exceeds the 1.0 km tolerance (1.70 km), so rows (c) and (e) are unavailable, and the driver was not silent: he gave the rider the reason for the reroute and the rider accepted it, which is the opposite of the uninformed-rider rows (b) and (d). The metered fare of 23.04 SGD should stand and the ruling should be NO_ACTION.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-05005", "EV-ADVIS-05005-01", "EV-TRIP-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-24", "POL-RD-21", "POL-RD-23"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-TRIP-05005].payload.events",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The deviation must be measured the way POL-RD-22 requires, on the platform's recorded distances - 13.8 km actual minus 12.1 km recommended = 1.70 km - and not on the GPS trace, which POL-RD-22 says is used only to show which route was taken and when and is never used to re-measure distance because a sparse trace understates a route's length. The driver does not dispute the 1.70 km figure; he asks only that the same measure, and no other, be applied to him, as the clause requires.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-05005", "EV-GPS-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-22"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-TRIP-05005].payload.derived.v7.deviation.deviation_km",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "On POL-RD-24's own arithmetic the rider cannot reach the 5.00 SGD he asks. The excess distance fare is the deviation distance times the per-km rate, without surge: 1.70 km x 0.92 SGD = 1.56 SGD. The two rows that can apply on a 1.70 km deviation where the rider is not treated as having agreed - row (b) and row (d) - both pay that same single figure and nothing else; no row of the matrix returns the whole 5.00 SGD, and none touches the base, time or platform components of the fare. A refund of 5.00 SGD would over-compensate the rider by 3.44 SGD, and the rider's round $5 figure is a number he has picked, not the excess distance fare the policy provides.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-FARE-05005", "EV-TRIP-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-24", "POL-RD-22"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-FARE-05005].payload.derived.money.excess_distance_fare_sgd",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The charge the rider is contesting is an ordinary metered fare, not a route-based surcharge: base 3.80 + distance 12.70 + time 5.64 + platform fee 0.90 = 23.04 SGD, with no surge (multiplier 1.0), no ERP and no waiting fee, and the fare was finalised at 10:38:14 on trip completion. The only component attributable to the extra 1.70 km is the 1.56 SGD distance increment, so even on the rider's best case the record supports no more than that single line and nothing in the remaining 21.48 SGD.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-FARE-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-24", "POL-GEN-03"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-FARE-05005].payload.total_charged",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    }
  ]
}
